From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ludovic Henry <git@ludovic.dev>
Date: Wed, 27 Aug 2026 00:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] tests: allow for interpreter startup in the --gil sample bound

test_longsleep records longsleep.py for 2s with --gil and asserts that at
most 10 samples come back, the point being that a sleeping thread holds the
GIL essentially never. But py-spy launches the interpreter itself, so the
recording also covers CPython's startup, and the GIL is legitimately held
throughout those imports. The bound is therefore really a statement about
how fast the machine starts an interpreter.

On the riscv64 runner startup is slow enough to overshoot it, marginally and
intermittently - 11 samples against a bound of 10, every one of them an
importlib/site/encodings frame:

    Frame(file='<frozen importlib._bootstrap>', name='_find_and_load', ...)
    Frame(file='<frozen site>', name='addsitepackages', ...)

At the default 100Hz that is 0.11s of startup. Raise the bound to 50 (0.5s)
so slower hardware has room, which still fails loudly if the GIL were held
for any real part of the 2s window (200 samples) rather than just startup.
Faster machines are unaffected - they simply stay far below either bound.

The companion assertion is untouched, and test_busyloop still requires >=95
GIL samples, so GIL detection itself remains covered.

Upstream-Status: To upstream [not yet submitted; the bound is hardware-speed-dependent rather than riscv64-specific and wants a maintainer's view on whether to raise it or to skip the interpreter's startup window instead]

Signed-off-by: Ludovic Henry <git@ludovic.dev>
---
diff --git a/tests/integration_test.py b/tests/integration_test.py
--- a/tests/integration_test.py
+++ b/tests/integration_test.py
@@ -68,7 +68,7 @@ class TestPyspy(unittest.TestCase):
         # running with the gil flag should have ~ no samples returned
         if GIL:
             profile = self._sample_process(_get_script("longsleep.py"), GIL)
             print(profile)
-            assert sum(profile.values()) <= 10
+            assert sum(profile.values()) <= 50
 
         # running with the idle flag should have > 95%  of samples in the sleep call
         profile = self._sample_process(_get_script("longsleep.py"), ["--idle"])
